    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** stands out as our premier option with an elite score of 69.33. Despite a challenging matchup against a left-handed arm, his role at the top of the order and the favorable park factor provide a high floor for stolen base production, which remains our primary objective. Joining him in the \"Strong\" tier is **Casey Schmitt** (63.92), whose projection is bolstered by a significant 11% platoon advantage, and **Kazuma Okamoto** (61.40), who benefits from a high-upside matchup against a pitcher with an xERA of 5.58, allowing him to capitalize on his #4 lineup position despite the neutral platoon environment.\n\nThe most difficult bench decision involved **Carson Benge**, who narrowly missed the cut at 43.14. While **Carson Benge** maintains a solid base projection, he ultimately fell short due to the platoon disadvantage against a left-handed starter and a lower projected efficiency compared to the rest of the active infield and utility slots. We have also opted to sit **Gary Sanchez** (38.05), **Dillon Dingler** (35.41), and **Lars Nootbaar** (33.98), all of whom fell below our 40.0 efficiency floor. These decisions reflect our commitment to game-cap management, specifically avoiding the risks posed by elite arms like Skenes, who suppresses the output of both **Christian Yelich** and the benched **Gary Sanchez**.\n\nRisks remain prevalent, particularly for **Trea Turner** and **Kyle Schwarber**, both of whom are locked into high-efficiency spots but must contend with a difficult matchup against Skubal (2.69 xERA). Furthermore, while **Colt Keith** (46.91) and **Caleb Durbin** (48.52) are active, their scores are suppressed by the presence of elite catchers behind the plate and high-level opposing starters. We are prioritizing the stolen base upside of **Pete Crow-Armstrong** and the order-derived advantages of **Yordan Alvarez** and **Junior Caminero** to stabilize the lineup against these tougher pitching matchups.",
